## Setup

This notebook is self-contained: install the dependencies, configure a provider key, start the default Jido runtime, and send one prompt through an AI agent. If you already completed [Your first agent](/docs/getting-started/first-agent), still run this notebook top to bottom because it defines its own module.

> **Required environment variable:** `OPENAI_API_KEY` (or `LB_OPENAI_API_KEY` when running inside Livebook). Set it before running the request cell. This notebook selects an explicit `openai:` model that matches that key.

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {{mix_dep:jido}},
  {{mix_dep:jido_ai}},
  {{mix_dep:req_llm}}
])

Logger.configure(level: :warning)

# Livebook imports can execute generated docs as doctests.
# Disable compiler docs until the current Jido Hex release drops the invalid signal_types/0 example.
Code.put_compiler_option(:docs, false)
```

## Configure credentials

In Livebook, store `OPENAI_API_KEY` as a secret. Livebook exposes that secret as `LB_OPENAI_API_KEY` inside the notebook, so this cell checks both names.

```elixir
openai_key = System.get_env("LB_OPENAI_API_KEY") || System.get_env("OPENAI_API_KEY")

configured? =
  if is_binary(openai_key) do
    ReqLLM.put_key(:openai_api_key, openai_key)
    true
  else
    IO.puts("Set OPENAI_API_KEY or LB_OPENAI_API_KEY before running the request cell.")
    false
  end
```

## Define a typed tool Action

A model-only agent can only generate text. To let an agent act — compute a value, return structured data, or call an API — you give it typed tool Actions. Every tool is a plain `Jido.Action` module: a typed `schema` declares its inputs, and a `run/2` callback performs the effect and returns `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, reason}`. Define the smallest useful one now.

```elixir
defmodule MyAgentApp.ComposeGreeting do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "compose_greeting",
    description: "Compose a greeting for a named person in a chosen tone.",
    schema: [
      name: [type: :string, required: true, doc: "Name of the person to greet"],
      tone: [
        type: {:in, ["warm", "formal", "playful"]},
        default: "warm",
        doc: "Tone of the greeting"
      ],
      enthusiasm: [
        type: :integer,
        default: 1,
        doc: "Exclamation marks to append, clamped to 0-3"
      ]
    ]

  @impl true
  def run(%{name: name, tone: tone, enthusiasm: enthusiasm}, _context) do
    salutation =
      case tone do
        "warm" -> "Hi"
        "formal" -> "Greetings"
        "playful" -> "Hey there"
      end

    marks = String.duplicate("!", clamp(enthusiasm, 0, 3))
    {:ok, %{greeting: "#{salutation}, #{name}#{marks}"}}
  end

  defp clamp(value, low, high), do: value |> max(low) |> min(high)
end
```

Test the effect directly, with no LLM in the loop. Calling `run/2` runs the Action's effect with the params you pass, so this cell works without a provider key:

```elixir
{:ok, composed} =
  MyAgentApp.ComposeGreeting.run(
    %{name: "Casey", tone: "playful", enthusiasm: 3},
    %{}
  )

IO.inspect(composed, label: "Composed greeting")
```

The `schema` is what both the runtime and the LLM read. Each parameter carries a `type` (`:string`, `:integer`, or an `{:in, [...]}` enum), an optional `default:` and `doc:`, and a `required:` flag. The `run/2` callback is the effect — it receives the validated params and a context map and returns a result. Because LLM tool calls arrive as JSON, enum values use strings (`"warm"`, not `:warm`). [AI Agent with Tools](/docs/learn/ai-agent-with-tools) builds the full ReAct tool-calling loop on this same Action type.

## Define the agent

`use Jido.AI.Agent` keeps the module declarative: name the agent, choose a model, list the tool Actions it may call, and provide a system prompt. This greeter lists the `compose_greeting` Action defined above, so it can both generate text and call a typed tool.

```elixir
defmodule MyAgentApp.Greeter do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "greeter",
    description: "Generates a friendly greeting",
    tools: [MyAgentApp.ComposeGreeting],
    model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini",
    system_prompt: """
    You are a friendly greeter.
    Generate a short, warm welcome message.
    One or two sentences maximum.
    """
end
```

`model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini"` names an explicit provider and model. The configured key is an OpenAI key, so the model must be an `openai:` model — a provider/model mismatch is the most common first-run failure. Model aliases like `:fast` are convenient, but each alias resolves to one fixed provider, so they are introduced later, once this explicit request succeeds. When you do adopt aliases, `llm_db` resolves each alias to a concrete provider and model and supplies its capabilities.

Before running the request, confirm the configured key provider matches the selected model provider:

```elixir
# Provider/model check: the key configured above is an OpenAI key, and the
# model is an explicit openai: model. A mismatch here is the most common
# first-run failure, so state both values up front.
IO.puts("Key provider:    openai")
IO.puts("Selected model:  openai:gpt-4o-mini")
```

## Start the runtime and agent

`Jido.start/0` is the notebook-friendly runtime helper. It is safe to call more than once, so rerunning this cell does not force you to restart the notebook. `Jido.start_agent/3` then starts your agent inside that runtime.

```elixir
{:ok, _} = Jido.start()
runtime = Jido.default_instance()
agent_id = "greeter-demo-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

{:ok, pid} = Jido.start_agent(runtime, MyAgentApp.Greeter, id: agent_id)
```

## Ask the agent

Send one prompt with `ask_sync/3`. The return value is `{:ok, text}` on success or `{:error, reason}` if the provider call fails.

```elixir
greeting =
  if configured? do
    MyAgentApp.Greeter.ask_sync(
      pid,
      "Say hello to someone just getting started with Jido.",
      timeout: 30_000
    )
  else
    {:skip, :no_openai_key}
  end

IO.inspect(greeting, label: "Greeting")
```

```elixir
case greeting do
  {:ok, text} ->
    IO.puts(text)

  {:error, reason} ->
    IO.puts("Greeting failed: #{inspect(reason)}")

  {:skip, :no_openai_key} ->
    IO.puts("Skipped request. Set OPENAI_API_KEY or LB_OPENAI_API_KEY to run it.")
end
```

## Inspect the last request

Show the success path first, then inspect the runtime state. `Jido.AgentServer.status/1` gives you the current snapshot for the running agent process.

```elixir
snapshot =
  case Jido.AgentServer.status(pid) do
    {:ok, status} ->
      %{
        done?: status.snapshot.done?,
        result: status.snapshot.result,
        model: status.snapshot.details[:model]
      }

    other ->
      other
  end

IO.inspect(snapshot, label: "Snapshot")
```

## What happened

1. `Jido.start/0` started the default runtime used for scripts and Livebook.
2. `Jido.start_agent/3` started `MyAgentApp.Greeter` as a supervised agent process.
3. `ask_sync/3` combined the system prompt and your message, then sent the request through `req_llm`.
4. `Jido.AgentServer.status/1` exposed the resulting snapshot so you could inspect the completed request.

The agent module stays free of provider-specific HTTP code. The runtime handles request execution, while your module stays focused on behavior.

## Moving this into a Mix project

Keep the agent module the same, add the dependencies to `mix.exs`, configure the provider key in `config/runtime.exs`, and supervise a `use Jido` module in your application:

```elixir
defmodule MyAgentApp.Jido do
  use Jido, otp_app: :my_agent_app
end
```

```elixir
children = [
  {MyAgentApp.Jido, name: MyAgentApp.Jido}
]
```

Then start agents against that named runtime:

```
{:ok, pid} = Jido.start_agent(MyAgentApp.Jido, MyAgentApp.Greeter, id: "greeter-1")
MyAgentApp.Greeter.ask_sync(pid, "Say hello to the production app.", timeout: 30_000)
```

## Troubleshooting

- **Missing key** — the request is skipped with a message. Set `OPENAI_API_KEY` (or `LB_OPENAI_API_KEY` in Livebook) and rerun the configure cell.
- **401 / invalid model** — confirm the model's provider matches your key. An OpenAI key needs an `openai:` model; an alias like `:fast` may resolve to a different provider.
- **429 / rate limit** — you hit the provider rate limit. Wait and retry, or lower concurrency.
- **Timeout** — `ask_sync/3` timed out. Raise the `timeout:` value, or check the provider's status.

These failures come from `req_llm`, the provider-request layer under `jido_ai`, not from core Jido.

